Recall request deadline + bounded worker drain, for the Hindsight HA add-on.

Applied in the Dockerfile with `patch -p1 -d /app/api` against the API package
COPY-ed from the digest-pinned upstream image (v0.10.2 / 5fc4ce20; the package
is an editable install, so the source lives at /app/api/hindsight_api). When
bumping HINDSIGHT_IMAGE, regenerate this patch against the new source — the
build fails loudly here if the context drifts.

Two changes, both no-ops unless their env var is set:

1. api/http.py: RecallDeadlineMiddleware, a pure-ASGI middleware installed in
   create_app between upstream's ClientDisconnectCancellationMiddleware and
   HttpObservabilityMiddleware. When HINDSIGHT_API_RECALL_HANDLER_TIMEOUT
   (seconds) > 0, every POST .../memories/recall runs under ONE
   asyncio.wait_for covering the WHOLE request — route-class bank-alias
   resolution, dependencies (auth, precheck, admission queue), body parsing,
   the engine call and all post-recall enrichment. On expiry the request task
   is cancelled and the client gets a fast 504 (if no response has started)
   instead of the server silently working past the consumer's own client
   timeout (observed on the N150: aborted clients left doomed work running,
   so queues grew without bound during busy evenings). Being inside the
   observability layer, the 504 is recorded in the HTTP request metrics.
   History: until add-on v0.4.x the deadline wrapped only the engine call.
   Rebasing onto v0.10.2, review found work escaping every narrower scope in
   turn — post-recall attachment lookups, the admission queue, and bank-alias
   resolution in the route class — so the deadline now bounds the request
   itself and covers anything upstream adds on the recall path later.
   Upstream (v0.9+) also cancels a recall when the client DISCONNECTS (499);
   this deadline is the complement — a typed 504 BEFORE the consumer's budget.

   metrics.py MetricsCollector.record_operation: the deadline cancels from
   OUTSIDE, so an engine call still in flight sees asyncio.CancelledError (a
   BaseException upstream's `except Exception` misses) and was recorded as a
   SUCCESS. An explicit CancelledError branch now records it as failed.
   (Client disconnects arrive as OperationCancelledError and stay excluded.)
   The operation metric keeps upstream's scope — the ENGINE outcome — so a
   deadline hit during post-recall enrichment after a successful engine call
   is visible as the request's 504 in the HTTP metrics, not as a failed
   engine operation.

   api/admission.py _acquire_unless_abandoned: the deadline can now cancel a
   request while it waits in upstream's admission queue. Upstream's race
   (acquire task vs. disconnect token) only cleaned up the token task on
   cancellation, so the orphaned acquire task later took a permit and never
   returned it -- each occurrence permanently shrank the recall lane until
   restart. On cancellation it now cancels the acquire task and returns the
   permit (via a done-callback) if one was already granted.

   build-smoke/recall_deadline_smoke.py gates all of this at build time
   through the PRODUCTION create_app (real middleware stack, route class and
   dependencies) with a stub engine, one slow step at a time.

2. api/http.py lifespan: make the worker poller drain timeout configurable
   via HINDSIGHT_API_WORKER_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT (was hardcoded 30.0s). With the
   HA Supervisor's default 10s docker-stop grace (config.yaml `timeout`), a
   30s drain guaranteed SIGKILL (exit 137) whenever consolidation/retain was
   in flight at stop time — e.g. every nightly cold backup. In-flight worker
   tasks are re-queued/retried after restart, so a short drain is safe.

--- a/hindsight_api/api/admission.py
+++ b/hindsight_api/api/admission.py
@@ -128,6 +128,19 @@
     gone = asyncio.ensure_future(abandoned.wait())
     try:
         done, _ = await asyncio.wait({acquire, gone}, timeout=timeout, return_when=asyncio.FIRST_COMPLETED)
+    except BaseException:
+        # HA add-on patch: the caller was cancelled while queued (the add-on's
+        # whole-request recall deadline cancels the request task from outside).
+        # Without this the orphaned `acquire` task would take the next free
+        # permit and never hand it back, draining the lane until restart. Cancel
+        # it, and if it already holds a permit (granted in the same tick) return
+        # it from a done-callback -- no await here, so a second cancellation
+        # cannot interrupt the cleanup.
+        acquire.cancel()
+        acquire.add_done_callback(
+            lambda task: None if task.cancelled() or task.exception() is not None else semaphore.release()
+        )
+        raise
     finally:
         gone.cancel()
 
--- a/hindsight_api/api/http.py
+++ b/hindsight_api/api/http.py
@@ -338,6 +338,71 @@
 _T = TypeVar("_T")
 
 
+_RECALL_DEADLINE_DETAIL = "Request timed out while searching memories. Try a shorter or more specific query."
+
+
+class RecallDeadlineMiddleware:
+    """End-to-end recall request deadline (HA add-on patch; pure ASGI).
+
+    When HINDSIGHT_API_RECALL_HANDLER_TIMEOUT (seconds) > 0, every
+    POST .../memories/recall request runs under ONE asyncio.wait_for from the
+    moment it enters this middleware until it finishes: route-class work (bank
+    alias resolution), dependencies (auth, precheck, admission queue), body
+    parsing, the engine call and all post-recall enrichment. On expiry the
+    request task is cancelled and, if no response has started yet, the client
+    gets a fast 504 instead of the server silently overrunning the consumer's
+    own budget. Bounding the whole request (not individual layers or awaits)
+    means work upstream adds anywhere on the recall path is covered
+    automatically. 0/unset passes requests straight through (upstream
+    behavior). Installed inside HttpObservabilityMiddleware so the 504 is
+    recorded in the HTTP request metrics.
+    """
+
+    def __init__(self, app) -> None:
+        self.app = app
+
+    async def __call__(self, scope, receive, send) -> None:
+        if (
+            scope["type"] != "http"
+            or scope.get("method") != "POST"
+            or not scope.get("path", "").endswith("/memories/recall")
+        ):
+            await self.app(scope, receive, send)
+            return
+        deadline = float(os.getenv("HINDSIGHT_API_RECALL_HANDLER_TIMEOUT", "0") or "0")
+        if deadline <= 0:
+            await self.app(scope, receive, send)
+            return
+
+        started = False
+
+        async def tracking_send(message) -> None:
+            nonlocal started
+            if message["type"] == "http.response.start":
+                # Set BEFORE awaiting: a cancellation landing mid-send must not
+                # make us emit a second response start.
+                started = True
+            await send(message)
+
+        try:
+            await asyncio.wait_for(self.app(scope, receive, tracking_send), timeout=deadline)
+        except (asyncio.TimeoutError, TimeoutError):
+            logger.error(f"[RECALL TIMEOUT] path={scope.get('path')} request deadline {deadline:g}s exceeded")
+            if started:
+                # The response already began; nothing valid can replace it. The
+                # server closes the incomplete response.
+                return
+            body = json.dumps({"detail": _RECALL_DEADLINE_DETAIL}).encode()
+            await send(
+                {
+                    "type": "http.response.start",
+                    "status": 504,
+                    "headers": [(b"content-type", b"application/json"), (b"content-length", str(len(body)).encode())],
+                }
+            )
+            await send({"type": "http.response.body", "body": body})
+
+
 async def run_cancellable_on_disconnect(
     http_request: Request,
     request_context: RequestContext,
